F-35A LIGHTNING II  (8 s; 1 scenes, 2 shots)
synopsis: Chief pilot Jon Beesley takes AA-1, the first F-35A Lightning II prototype, off from Lockheed Martin in Fort Worth, Texas, at 12:44 p.m. Central Standard Time on December 15, 2006. Indistinct beneath its dark canopy, he maintains a straight, nose-high departure with the landing gear extended while successive labels and narration identify its single engine and conventional takeoff. The aircraft continues climbing above the runway as this eight-second excerpt of the inaugural thirty-five-minute development test flight ends.
theme: Quiet confidence expressed through precise, measured progress.

1. EXT. LOCKHEED MARTIN IN FORT WORTH, TEXAS — DAY   0–8 s
   synopsis: AA-1 lifts off above the runway with Jon Beesley indistinct beneath its dark canopy and all three landing-gear assemblies extended. Its straight departure illustrates the single-engine configuration and conventional takeoff during an eight-second excerpt of its inaugural development test flight.
   situation: At 12:44 p.m. Central Standard Time on December 15, 2006, AA-1 lifts clear of the runway at Lockheed Martin in Fort Worth and continues a straight, wings-level climb with all landing gear extended; Jon Beesley remains seated beneath the dark canopy, with daylight, runway, grass margins and the low open horizon outside.
   stage: Origin is the runway centreline at the lift-off point. Positive x runs horizontally along the runway toward its far end; positive y is right when facing positive x; z is height above runway pavement. Aircraft coordinates refer to its fuselage centre, with its lowest extended tires 2.35 metres below that reference. Both cameras remain at negative y. The aircraft travels straight at 80 metres per second horizontally with a steady 10-degree climb angle.; x toward runway
   tone: Calm, precise and quietly consequential.
   ambience: Continuous single-engine jet roar with light airfield wind; no voices or other aircraft.
   captions: [0s] "F-35A Lightning II"  [2s] "One engine"  [5s] "Conventional takeoff"
   line: jon-beesley aboard aa-1 and the runway centreline ahead; camera side: The grass-margin side corresponding to negative y; both cameras remain on this side of the runway centreline.
